// lib/proc.wz: proc.alive(pid) used to answer false, silently, for pid <= 0 -- a "not
// alive" that looks like a real answer but was never an answer to a real question. 0 and
// a negative pid address a process GROUP to kill(2), not a single process, so treating
// that as "checked, and it is not running" can hide a caller bug (an uninitialised or
// unset pid field) behind what looks like ordinary control flow.
//
// Every known caller (lib/proc.wz's own test, and the applications built on it)
// already guards pid > 0 itself before calling proc.alive, so this must be the caller's
// contract to keep -- the same shape as proc.arg's "too many arguments" check -- and the
// fix is io.fatal, not a quieter false.
//
// Must stop at run time with exit status 1 and the message below; take the
// `if pid <= 0 then io.fatal(...)` line out of proc.alive and this prints nothing and
// exits 0 instead.
import io;
import proc;
begin
  io.puts(STDOUT, "calling proc.alive(0)\n");
  if proc.alive(0) then io.puts(STDOUT, "alive\n") else io.puts(STDOUT, "not alive\n");
end.
